                <section id="section-patricio">
    <header>
        <h1>Part III: The Juan Patricio Case</h1>
        <p>Yucatán Peninsula</p>
    </header>

    <div class="card" id="patricio-overview">
        <span class="badge yucatan">An Overview</span>
        <h3>Dispossession in the Yucatán Peninsula</h3>

        <p>
            Juan Patricio's story demonstrates a genealogy of how possession and
            dispossession were and are apprehended and lived, which is to say
            accounts that unsettle the values we presumptively impute to possession
            and dispossession, including the values we impute to struggles against
            dispossession.
        </p>

        <p>
            In fact, the dispossessed did not always challenge their dispossession by
            claiming prior possession of their bodies, their lands, or their labor,
            or by demanding renewed possession as a singular remedy. In many cases,
            the dispossessed placed a much higher value on shaping and maintaining
            their social relations both with other dispossessed people and with the
            dispossessors they daily confront than they did on gaining or regaining
            possessions like land, labor, or even their own bodies.
        </p>

        <blockquote class="patricio-aside">
            <p>
                “Throughout the legajo, Juan Patricio shows himself to be much less
                interested in gaining or regaining any possessions than he is in
                animating the social relations he has with Black and Indigenous
                people, as well as both Spaniards and Creoles.”
            </p>
        </blockquote>

        <p class="patricio-quote-attribution">
            —David Kazanjian,
            <em>“Ante-Possession: A History of Dispossession’s Present.”</em>
        </p>

        <p>
            Thus, highlighting differences among cultures in defining what is
            “possessed” and the value placed on those possessions, in 1696 a case in
            Yucatán was presented: “Certified copy of criminal proceedings brought
            by Doña Isabel Solis, resident of Yucatán, against Juan Patricio slave,
            for having beaten Don Ignacio de Esquivel, priest.” In this story, Juan
            Patricio and Fabiana Pech help us chart a history of the not-yet-present
            of our own <strong>ante-possessive</strong> politics.
        </p>

        <blockquote class="patricio-aside">
            <p><strong>Ante-Possession:</strong></p>

            <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem; font-style: normal; font-size: 0.95rem;">
                The social relations that exist before, against, or alongside racial
                capitalist modes of possession. By shifting attention away from
                ownership and toward relationships, ante-possession reveals that
                possession itself must be created before dispossession can take
                place.
            </p>
        </blockquote>
    </div>

